# Swift/iOS testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Swift
Testing (the current default for new test code) and legacy XCTest, where
the project has not yet migrated. Applies only to `*.swift` files.

## Swift Testing vs XCTest

- For new test code on a toolchain that supports it, prefer the Swift
  Testing framework (`import Testing`, `@Test` functions, `#expect`/
  `#require` macros) over XCTest — it gives clearer failure messages
  (`#expect` captures the evaluated sub-expressions), native
  parameterized tests, and tests run in parallel by default.
- Swift Testing does not yet replace XCTest everywhere: UI automation
  (`XCUITest`) and performance tests (`XCTMetric`/`measure`) still run
  on XCTest, and an existing XCTest suite does not need a wholesale
  rewrite just to adopt Swift Testing — the two frameworks coexist in
  the same target. Match whichever the project's existing test target
  already uses for a given kind of test; introduce Swift Testing for new
  unit-test coverage rather than mixing conventions within one file.
- A Swift Testing function is `@Test func name() async throws { ... }`
  (not `test`-prefixed, no `XCTestCase` subclass needed); assert with
  `#expect(condition)` for a check that should record a failure and
  continue, or `try #require(optional)` for one that must stop the test
  immediately if it fails (the Swift Testing equivalent of
  `XCTUnwrap`/`XCTAssert...` followed by an early return).

## Structure and naming

- One `@Test` (or `XCTest` method) per behavior being verified; a
  descriptive test name (`@Test("returns nil for an empty cart")` or a
  method named `test_returnsNil_whenCartIsEmpty`) over `test1`/`test2`.
- Use Swift Testing's `arguments:` parameterization for the same
  assertion repeated over several inputs, instead of a hand-rolled loop
  inside one test function or several copy-pasted test functions that
  differ only by input.
- Group related tests with a `struct`/`enum` namespace and Swift
  Testing's `@Suite`, or an `XCTestCase` subclass for legacy code,
  matching whatever the target already does.

## Mocking at the protocol boundary

- Mock external dependencies — network clients, persistence
  (`UserDefaults`, Core Data, a database layer), platform services
  (location, notifications) — at a protocol boundary the production code
  already depends on, never by mocking the type under test's own
  internal collaborators or reaching into its private state.
- Define (or reuse) a narrow protocol for the dependency (`protocol
  OrderClient { func fetchOrder(id: String) async throws -> Order }`),
  inject a real conforming type in production and a test double
  conforming to the same protocol in tests — the type under test never
  needs to know it is under test.
- Do not mock one layer too deep (stubbing a private helper method, or
  a concrete `URLSession` call inside a class instead of the protocol
  that wraps it) — that couples the test to an implementation detail and
  breaks on a harmless refactor instead of only on an actual behavior
  change.

## Async and concurrency in tests

- Mark a test `async` and `await` the async call under test directly —
  Swift Testing and modern `XCTestCase` both support `async` test
  methods natively; do not reach for an `XCTestExpectation`/semaphore to
  bridge an `async` API into a synchronous-looking test unless the
  project's minimum toolchain predates async test support.
- Never synchronize with a fixed delay (`Thread.sleep`/`Task.sleep` used
  as a guess at "enough time") to wait for concurrent work to finish —
  `await` the async call, join a `TaskGroup`, or await an
  `XCTestExpectation` with a real fulfillment signal instead of a timed
  guess.
- Annotate a test `@MainActor` when it exercises `@MainActor`-isolated
  view-model/state code, so the test itself runs on the same actor its
  subject requires.

## Determinism

- A test must not depend on real network access, the real Keychain, or
  wall-clock time; inject a fake/in-memory conforming type at the
  protocol boundary and, for time-dependent logic, inject a clock/date
  provider instead of calling `Date()` directly inside the code under
  test.
- A bug fix gets a regression test that fails before the fix and passes
  after; new behavior gets new test coverage in the same change.
